# A clock — SSC3-3d

`nanoTime()` is the language's name; `io.nanoTime` is the prim's, and it is v2's own name for the
same thing. The bridge emits prim names verbatim, so a different name would be a program that runs
on the executor and is refused on the bridge — invariant I-3, from the one decision this feature
actually had to make.

**THE VALUE IS NEVER PRINTED, and that is a rule rather than a habit.** `exec-gate.sh` runs every
fixture on the executor AND on the v2 bridge and requires identical output; a clock is the first
prim whose result differs on every call, so printing one would make that gate fail forever and for
the wrong reason. What is printed here is what a clock GUARANTEES — that it does not go backwards,
that it is positive, and that a difference is a number — none of which depends on when it runs.

```scalascript
val t0 = nanoTime()
val t1 = nanoTime()
val elapsed = t1 - t0

println(t0 > 0)
println(t1 >= t0)
println(elapsed >= 0)
```
